 "title": "Complex Inversions",
 "description": "Inversion with a noun subject, and after certain adverbs.",
 "items": [
  {
   "kind": "flashcard",
   "word": "L'inversion complexe",
   "type": "Rule",
   "translation": "The noun subject stays in front, and a matching pronoun is added after the verb",
   "exampleFr": "Marie vient-elle demain ?",
   "exampleEn": "Is Marie coming tomorrow?",
   "id": "001"
  },
  {
   "kind": "flashcard",
   "word": "-t- euphonique",
   "type": "Rule",
   "translation": "Insert -t- when the verb ends in a vowel before il, elle, on",
   "exampleFr": "Paul a-t-il téléphoné ?",
   "exampleEn": "Did Paul phone?",
   "id": "002"
  },
  {
   "kind": "flashcard",
   "word": "Après peut-être, sans doute, ainsi, aussi, à peine",
   "type": "Rule",
   "translation": "In formal writing these adverbs at the start trigger inversion",
   "exampleFr": "Peut-être viendra-t-il. À peine était-il arrivé que…",
   "exampleEn": "Perhaps he will come. He had hardly arrived when…",
   "id": "003"
  },
  {
   "kind": "flashcard",
   "word": "Les incises",
   "type": "Rule",
   "translation": "Reporting clauses inside or after a quotation are inverted",
   "exampleFr": "« Je reviendrai », dit-elle.",
   "exampleEn": "“I will come back,” she said.",
   "id": "004"
  },
  {
   "kind": "flashcard",
   "word": "Avec je",
   "type": "Rule",
   "translation": "Inversion with je is rare; only a few forms are used",
   "exampleFr": "Puis-je ? Suis-je ? Ai-je ? Dois-je ?",
   "exampleEn": "May I? Am I? Have I? Must I?",
   "hint": "“Peux-je” does not exist: say “puis-je”",
   "id": "005"
  },
